{{also|omer|Homer|Homér}}
==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈhəʊmə/|lang=en}}
*: {{rhymes|əʊmə(r)|lang=en}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{etyl|he|en}} {{m/he|חומר|dwv=חֹמֶר|tr=khómer|gloss=[[clay]], [[heap]], [[large]] [[unit]] of [[volume]]}}.

====Alternative forms====
* {{l|en|chomer}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|historical|_|units of measure}} A [[former]] [[Hebrew]] [[unit]] of [[dry]] [[volume]], [[about]] [[equal]] to 230{{nbsp}}[[L]] or 6½<!--US-->{{nbsp}}[[bushel]]s.
#* '''1769''', [[w:King James Version of the Bible|Bible (KJV)]], [[w:Book of Ezekiel|Ezekiel]], [[s:Bible (King James)/Ezekiel#Chapter 45|45:11]]:
#*: The [[ephah]] and the [[bath]] shall be of one measure, that the [[bath]] may contain the tenth part of an homer, and the [[ephah]] the tenth part of an '''homer''': the measure thereof shall be after the '''homer'''.
#* {{quote-text|year=1922|author=James Joyce|title=Ulysses|passage=Head up! For every newbegotten thou shalt gather thy '''homer''' of ripe wheat.}}
# {{lb|en|historical|_|units of measure}} {{altname|cor|lang=en|nodot=a}}: [[approximately]] the [[same]] [[volume]] as a [[liquid]] [[measure]].
#* '''1769''', [[w:King James Version of the Bible|Bible (KJV)]], [[w:Book of Ezekiel|Ezekiel]], [[s:Bible (King James)/Ezekiel#Chapter 45|45:11]]:
#*: The [[ephah]] and the [[bath]] shall be of one measure, that the [[bath]] may contain the tenth part of an '''homer''', and the [[ephah]] the tenth part of an homer: the measure thereof shall be after the '''homer'''.

=====Usage notes=====
In English, sometimes confounded with the much smaller {{m|en|omer}}.

=====Synonyms=====
* {{l|en|cor}}, {{l|en|kor}}

=====Meronyms=====
* {{sense|dry measure}} {{l|en|cab}}, {{l|en|kab}} {{qualifier|{{frac|1|180}} homer}}; {{l|en|omer}}, {{l|en|issaron}} {{qualifier|{{frac|1|100}} homer}}; {{l|en|seah}} {{qualifier|{{frac|1|30}} homer}}; {{l|en|ephah}} {{qualifier|{{frac|1|10}} homer}}; {{l|en|lethek}}, {{l|en|lethech}} {{qualifier|½ homer}}
* {{sense|liquid measure}} ''See'' '''{{l|en|cor}}'''

===Etymology 2===
From [[home]].

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|baseball}} A four-base hit; a [[home run]]
#: ''The first baseman hit a '''homer''' to lead off the ninth.''
# A [[homing pigeon]]
#: ''Each of the pigeon fanciers released a '''homer''' at the same time.''
# {{lb|en|sports}} A person who is extremely [[devoted]] to his favorite team.
#: ''Joe is such a '''homer''' that he would never boo the Hometown Hobos, even if they are in last place in the league.''

